Each style has a review sheet generated from the candidate:

- the typography specimen, the every-block fixture, every chart type, the map, flow and pivot table, and the flagship, at 320, 390 and 1440 px and as PDF;
- the same content in the other four styles beside it, so structural differences show: outlines and shading, legend and title positions, Scientific's captions below charts in boxes;
- the style's declared structure (where legends and titles sit, outlines or none) verified against the geometry model, and STY-52's measured checks;
- entrance clips in that style;
- STY-51's three answers.

You review a sheet when a style is created and when its tokens or structure change; packets that change any capture in a style show the sheet's diff. A mechanical check flags two styles whose structural declarations are identical; distinctiveness beyond that is your judgement. Once you accept a style, later changes to it come one by one through the design log (DRAFT-2).

A chart type merges only with all of these, and `learn`, the docs and every count list it only then (DEL-18):

- schema, `learn` entry and a golden input with sourced data, plus edge fixtures: empty, single value, negative, missing, extremes, dense;
- its row in FEA-CH-69's table, with a test that each listed check fires;
- geometry checks: no clipping or collision at 320, 390 and 1440 px in every style;
- values at the author's precision in readouts, Table, CSV and exports (DEL-52, FEA-CH-37);
- its interaction policy (FEA-CH-39) with pointer and keyboard tests;
- its motion spec with motion tests (DRAFT-8);
- its accessible summary (FEA-CH-55);
- vector output in a PDF pagination fixture (FEA-RP-43);
- captures: one case per type, per style, at 390 and 1440 px (DEL-27);
- a local comparison against the libraries that define the type (ggplot2, Plotly, ECharts, Highcharts, Power BI) on the same data (ARC-64), shown on its first design card;
- an entry in the all-types performance fixture (PRF-07).

The type list compiled from those libraries arrives as feature cards per family, so each type is accepted before it is built.
